## Task #22: String == on values flowing through a Result destructure
## and through `String`-typed parameters must use strcmp, not pointer-eq.
##
## Before the fix, function parameters annotated `s: String` were typed
## `Ty::Class { name: "String" }` (resolve/items.rs::resolve_class
## stomps the primitive TypeAlias kind during class-body resolution).
## The codegen `is_string_mir_ty` helper only recognised the canonical
## `Ty::String` form, so any compare where BOTH sides came from such a
## parameter fell back to integer pointer-equality — byte-identical
## strings compared unequal silently. This impacted every URL match,
## header lookup, and response comparison in real server code.
##
## The fixture exercises the two surface shapes that previously failed:
##   1. A `String` parameter compared with a `String` parameter inside a
##      helper called from a `match Ok(s) -> check(s, expected)` arm.
##      (Both compare operands are `Ty::Class { "String" }`.)
##   2. A direct `match Ok(s) -> s == expected_param` from a String-typed
##      param compared against another String-typed param.

def make_result(payload: String) -> Result[String, Int]
  Ok(payload)
end

def check_strings(s: String, needle: String) -> Int
  if s == needle
    1
  else
    0
  end
end

def main
  let want = "hello"
  let needle = "hello"
  let r = match make_result(want)
    Ok(s)  -> check_strings(s, needle)
    Err(_) -> 0
  end
  if r == 1
    puts "match"
  else
    puts "fail"
  end
end
